expected_output:
Notice that the two fractions have the same denominator, so we can add them. Addition is commutative, so we can rearrange the terms to get  \begin{align*}
\frac{k-3}{2} +\frac{3k+1}{2}+3k+1 &=\frac{4k-2}{2}+3k+1 \\
&=2k-1+3k+1 \\
&=\boxed{5k}.\end{align*}

question:
In how many ways can seven beads of distinct colors be put on the hexagonal grid shown, if reflections and rotations of an arrangement are considered equivalent?

[asy]
size(50);
dot((0,0));
dot((1,0));
dot((-1,0));
dot((.5,sqrt(3)/2));
dot((.5,-sqrt(3)/2));
dot((-.5,sqrt(3)/2));
dot((-.5,-sqrt(3)/2));
[/asy]
